**Proposition 8.2 (source-history recovery leaves this ambiguity).** Supplying the entire actual source history to an external evaluator, together with the coarse interface of Convention 1.1, determines neither the configured complete laws nor the exact p configuration risk in Theorem 6.3.

**Proof.** An original actual source history records letters and original control operations; it does not contain the private stochastic transition draws or the installed $B$ row. By Theorem 6.1 every family member has the same distribution of those source histories and the same actual label row. Theorem 5.1 additionally keeps the paired arrays, all emissions and all configured short future laws equal. Consequently even giving an external analyst the exact same entire acquired source history cannot distinguish the family members using only those other coarse data. That analyst's original target $T_h^\mu$ is the same, while the generated decoder laws in (5.1) differ.

This observation does not install an archive at runtime. It holds after hypothetically supplying the entire actual history to an external evaluator, so a representation that merely recovers that history cannot resolve this particular kernel ambiguity. A putative transfer from sparse transport must provide a relation identifying the relevant installed kernel or preserving each individual generator law. For the exact p risk, Theorem 6.3 gives two different values for these same interface data and any such same source history. Merely transporting source replies does not supply that relation. $\square$

